1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,968
This is ECE 358, video 11. The topic is TCP congestion control. It covers Chapter 3, slides 73 to 89.

2
00:00:12,418 --> 00:00:20,631
After this video, you can explain congestion, AIMD, slow start, S S thresh and congestion avoidance.

3
00:00:21,081 --> 00:00:26,841
You can apply the Tahoe and Reno rules for a timeout and for triple duplicate ACKs.

4
00:00:27,291 --> 00:00:36,635
You can read a C wind plot round by round. This is the 2016 final question, and problem set 3, problem 4.

5
00:00:37,085 --> 00:00:44,979
And you can calculate the TCP rate, and the average throughput of Reno, 3 W over 4 RTT.

6
00:00:45,429 --> 00:00:48,074
Three senders send data at the same time.

7
00:00:48,524 --> 00:00:50,807
All their packets go through one router.

8
00:00:51,257 --> 00:00:56,099
The router sends the packets out on one link. The rate of this link is R.

9
00:00:56,549 --> 00:01:02,011
When the senders together send slower than R, the output buffer stays almost empty.

10
00:01:02,461 --> 00:01:07,922
When they send faster than R, packets wait in the output buffer. The queue grows.

11
00:01:08,372 --> 00:01:10,527
So the queueing delay becomes long.

12
00:01:10,977 --> 00:01:15,265
When the buffer is full, the router drops the next packet that arrives.

13
00:01:15,715 --> 00:01:18,445
This is packet loss from buffer overflow.

14
00:01:18,895 --> 00:01:28,090
This is congestion: too many sources send too much data too fast for the network to handle. It shows as long delays and packet loss.

15
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:39,953
The solution is congestion control. The senders notice the congestion and control their data rate. The slide calls it a service to the network, for the common good of all.

16
00:01:40,403 --> 00:01:46,142
Flow control solves a different problem. One sender sends too fast for one receiver.

17
00:01:46,592 --> 00:01:49,173
The receive buffer of the receiver fills up.

18
00:01:49,623 --> 00:01:57,367
Congestion control solves a problem in the network. Many senders together send too fast for a router and its link.

19
00:01:57,817 --> 00:02:01,145
The full buffer is in the router, not in the receiver.

20
00:02:01,595 --> 00:02:10,705
Compare the two. Flow control: one sender is too fast for one receiver. Congestion control: too many senders send too fast.

21
00:02:11,155 --> 00:02:18,067
Flow control protects the receiver. Congestion control protects the network: the routers and the links.

22
00:02:18,517 --> 00:02:23,786
Flow control uses R wind. The receiver sends R wind to the sender.

23
00:02:24,236 --> 00:02:30,593
Congestion control uses C wind. The sender keeps C wind. You will see C wind next.

24
00:02:31,043 --> 00:02:39,342
There are two basic approaches. The first is end-to-end congestion control. The network gives no explicit feedback.

25
00:02:39,792 --> 00:02:43,611
The sender sees only its data and the ACKs that come back.

26
00:02:44,061 --> 00:02:49,095
So the sender infers congestion from the loss and the delay that it observes.

27
00:02:49,545 --> 00:02:52,041
This is the approach that TCP takes.

28
00:02:52,491 --> 00:03:02,070
The second approach is network-assisted congestion control. A congested router gives direct feedback to the hosts whose flows pass through it.

29
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:07,085
The red arrows carry this explicit congestion information to the hosts.

30
00:03:07,535 --> 00:03:12,122
The feedback can give the congestion level, or it can set the sending rate.

31
00:03:12,572 --> 00:03:21,169
Examples are TCP E C N, which uses bits in the TCP header, A T M, and the deck bit protocol.

32
00:03:21,619 --> 00:03:26,995
Here is the sender's sequence number space. The green bytes are already acknowledged.

33
00:03:27,445 --> 00:03:31,755
The orange bytes are sent, but not yet acknowledged. They are in flight.

34
00:03:32,205 --> 00:03:35,725
Last byte acked is the end of the acknowledged bytes.

35
00:03:36,175 --> 00:03:40,228
Last byte sent is the end of the bytes that the sender has sent.

36
00:03:40,678 --> 00:03:45,819
So last byte sent minus last byte acked is the number of bytes in flight.

37
00:03:46,269 --> 00:03:54,013
C wind is the congestion window. It is a window over the sequence number space. It starts at last byte acked.

38
00:03:54,463 --> 00:03:59,626
The sender can also send the blue bytes, because they are still inside C wind.

39
00:04:00,076 --> 00:04:14,689
So the TCP sender limits its transmission. Last byte sent minus last byte acked must be at most the minimum of C wind and R wind. This equation joins congestion control and flow control.

40
00:04:15,139 --> 00:04:23,417
C wind is kept by the sender. The sender changes it when it detects congestion: triple duplicate ACKs or a timeout.

41
00:04:23,867 --> 00:04:28,965
R wind comes from the receiver, in the segment header. It is for flow control.

42
00:04:29,415 --> 00:04:35,901
How fast does a TCP sender send? Roughly, it sends C wind bytes into the network.

43
00:04:36,351 --> 00:04:41,364
Then it waits about one RTT, until the ACKs for these bytes come back.

44
00:04:41,814 --> 00:04:47,617
Then it sends the next C wind bytes. So the sender sends one window per RTT.

45
00:04:48,067 --> 00:04:54,211
So the TCP rate is about C wind divided by RTT, in bytes per second.

46
00:04:54,661 --> 00:04:59,738
Here is the example from slide 80. C wind is 20 MSS.

47
00:05:00,188 --> 00:05:03,772
One MSS is 1500 bytes.

48
00:05:04,222 --> 00:05:07,209
The RTT is 200 ms.

49
00:05:07,659 --> 00:05:13,867
One window is 20 times 1500 bytes. That is 30 thousand bytes.

50
00:05:14,317 --> 00:05:22,039
One byte is 8 bits. So one window is 30 thousand times 8, which is 240 thousand bits.

51
00:05:22,489 --> 00:05:29,231
One second is 1000 ms. So 200 ms is 0 point 2 seconds.

52
00:05:29,681 --> 00:05:40,987
The sender sends one window per RTT. 240 thousand bits divided by 0 point 2 seconds is 1 point 2 million bits per second.

53
00:05:41,437 --> 00:05:49,693
1 Mbps is 10 to the power 6 bits per second. So the rate is 1 point 2 Mbps.

54
00:05:50,143 --> 00:05:54,239
So to change the sending rate, TCP changes C wind.

55
00:05:54,689 --> 00:06:06,508
TCP uses AIMD: additive increase, multiplicative decrease. Additive increase: C wind grows by 1 MSS every RTT.

56
00:06:06,958 --> 00:06:13,123
C wind grows until the sender detects a loss. Here the loss comes at C wind 14.

57
00:06:13,573 --> 00:06:22,448
Multiplicative decrease: at a loss event, the sender cuts C wind, for example in half. 14 divided by 2 is 7.

58
00:06:22,898 --> 00:06:31,773
Then additive increase starts again. The result is a sawtooth. The sender probes for bandwidth, backs off at a loss, and probes again.

59
00:06:32,223 --> 00:06:46,004
Why AIMD? AIMD is a distributed, asynchronous algorithm. Each sender acts alone. It has been shown to optimize congested flow rates network wide, with stability.

60
00:06:46,454 --> 00:06:56,459
How does a TCP sender detect a loss? There are two ways. The first is triple duplicate ACKs. Host A sends segments 1 to 5.

61
00:06:56,909 --> 00:06:59,064
Segment 2 is lost on the way.

62
00:06:59,514 --> 00:07:07,343
Host B gets segment 1 and sends ACK 2. ACK 2 means: the next segment that I expect is segment 2.

63
00:07:07,793 --> 00:07:18,076
Segments 3, 4 and 5 arrive, but segment 2 is still missing. For each one, Host B sends ACK 2 again. These are 3 duplicate ACKs.

64
00:07:18,526 --> 00:07:24,841
After 3 duplicate ACKs, Host A resends segment 2. It does not wait for the timer.

65
00:07:25,291 --> 00:07:30,261
The second way is a timeout. Segment 2 is lost, and no ACK comes back.

66
00:07:30,711 --> 00:07:33,143
The timer of segment 2 runs out.

67
00:07:33,593 --> 00:07:35,812
Then Host A resends segment 2.

68
00:07:36,262 --> 00:07:42,470
The reaction depends on the way. On triple duplicate ACKs, Reno cuts C wind in half.

69
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:47,315
On a timeout, Reno and Tahoe cut C wind to 1 MSS.

70
00:07:47,765 --> 00:07:56,810
A new connection does not know the available bandwidth. So it starts with slow start. At the start, C wind is 1 MSS.

71
00:07:57,260 --> 00:08:04,321
S S thresh, the slow start threshold, has an initial value, for example 64 kilobytes.

72
00:08:04,771 --> 00:08:09,081
In round 1, C wind is 1. The sender sends one segment.

73
00:08:09,531 --> 00:08:19,600
One ACK comes back. For every ACK received, the sender adds 1 MSS to C wind. So C wind becomes 1 plus 1, which is 2.

74
00:08:20,050 --> 00:08:24,167
In round 2, C wind is 2. The sender sends two segments.

75
00:08:24,617 --> 00:08:29,247
Two ACKs come back. So C wind becomes 2 plus 2, which is 4.

76
00:08:29,697 --> 00:08:34,134
In round 3, C wind is 4. The sender sends four segments.

77
00:08:34,584 --> 00:08:39,299
Four ACKs come back. So C wind becomes 4 plus 4, which is 8.

78
00:08:39,749 --> 00:08:45,018
So adding 1 MSS for every ACK doubles C wind every RTT.

79
00:08:45,468 --> 00:08:54,300
The initial rate is slow, but it ramps up exponentially fast. Slow start continues until C wind reaches S S thresh.

80
00:08:54,750 --> 00:09:05,161
When does the exponential growth stop? It stops when C wind reaches S S thresh. In this example from slide 82, S S thresh is 8.

81
00:09:05,611 --> 00:09:10,688
In rounds 1 to 4, slow start doubles C wind: 1, 2, 4, 8.

82
00:09:11,138 --> 00:09:15,362
In round 4, C wind is 8. It has reached S S thresh.

83
00:09:15,812 --> 00:09:25,028
So the sender switches to linear growth, the additive increase of AIMD. C wind becomes 9, 10, 11 and 12.

84
00:09:25,478 --> 00:09:29,937
After round 8, at C wind 12, the sender detects a loss.

85
00:09:30,387 --> 00:09:39,965
S S thresh is not a constant. It is a variable. On a loss event, the sender sets S S thresh to half of C wind just before the loss.

86
00:09:40,415 --> 00:09:44,767
Here the new S S thresh is 12 divided by 2, which is 6.

87
00:09:45,217 --> 00:09:49,036
Here S S thresh is 4, and C wind has reached 4.

88
00:09:49,486 --> 00:09:53,518
So the connection is in the congestion avoidance phase, C A.

89
00:09:53,968 --> 00:09:57,701
The sender sends 4 segments, and 4 ACKs come back.

90
00:09:58,151 --> 00:10:06,727
In C A, the sender adds 1 MSS to C wind only when all the segments of the previous C wind are acknowledged.

91
00:10:07,177 --> 00:10:14,516
So after the 4 ACKs, C wind becomes 4 plus 1, which is 5. The sender sends 5 segments.

92
00:10:14,966 --> 00:10:22,219
After those 5 ACKs, C wind becomes 5 plus 1, which is 6. The sender sends 6 segments.

93
00:10:22,669 --> 00:10:26,659
So in slow start, C wind doubles every RTT.

94
00:10:27,109 --> 00:10:33,423
In congestion avoidance, C wind grows by only 1 MSS every RTT.

95
00:10:33,873 --> 00:10:45,351
This table is question 47 of the transport part of the question bank. Fill in the new S S thresh and C wind for each loss event. Pause the video and try it.

96
00:10:45,801 --> 00:10:54,910
TCP Tahoe, triple duplicate ACKs: S S thresh becomes C wind over 2, and C wind becomes 1 MSS.

97
00:10:55,360 --> 00:11:01,696
TCP Tahoe, timeout: the same. Tahoe always sets C wind to 1 MSS.

98
00:11:02,146 --> 00:11:09,933
TCP Reno, timeout: S S thresh becomes C wind over 2, and C wind becomes 1 MSS.

99
00:11:10,383 --> 00:11:21,327
TCP Reno, triple duplicate ACKs: this is the one different cell. S S thresh equals C wind over 2, and C wind equals S S thresh.

100
00:11:21,777 --> 00:11:32,998
When C wind is set to 1 MSS, the sender is in slow start again. This happens on a timeout, and in Tahoe also on triple duplicate ACKs.

101
00:11:33,448 --> 00:11:42,515
Reno on triple duplicate ACKs cuts C wind in half and stays in congestion avoidance. Then C wind grows linearly.

102
00:11:42,965 --> 00:11:49,706
Duplicate ACKs show that the network can still deliver some segments. So Reno only halves C wind.

103
00:11:50,156 --> 00:11:59,521
Slide 85 compares Reno and Tahoe. S S thresh starts at 8. In rounds 1 to 8, both variants behave the same.

104
00:11:59,971 --> 00:12:05,198
After round 8, at C wind 12, the sender gets triple duplicate ACKs.

105
00:12:05,648 --> 00:12:09,765
Both variants set S S thresh to 12 over 2, which is 6.

106
00:12:10,215 --> 00:12:15,911
Tahoe sets C wind to 1 MSS. Then slow start gives 1, 2, 4.

107
00:12:16,361 --> 00:12:24,980
The next doubling would give 4 times 2, which is 8. But 8 is more than S S thresh, 6. So C wind stops at 6.

108
00:12:25,430 --> 00:12:30,187
Then Tahoe grows C wind by 1 each round: 7, 8, 9.

109
00:12:30,637 --> 00:12:35,480
Reno sets C wind to S S thresh, which is 6, in round 9.

110
00:12:35,930 --> 00:12:40,922
Then Reno grows linearly: 7, 8, up to 12 in round 15.

111
00:12:41,372 --> 00:12:49,692
The Reno line is always above the Tahoe line after the loss. So Reno sends more data in rounds 9 to 15.

112
00:12:50,142 --> 00:12:58,611
What is the average throughput of TCP Reno? Ignore slow start. Assume that the sender always has data to send.

113
00:12:59,061 --> 00:13:03,093
W is the window size, in bytes, where a loss occurs.

114
00:13:03,543 --> 00:13:10,541
Each loss comes from triple duplicate ACKs. So C wind drops from W to W over 2.

115
00:13:10,991 --> 00:13:17,071
In each tooth, C wind climbs in a straight line from W over 2 back to W.

116
00:13:17,521 --> 00:13:25,094
The average of a straight line is the middle of its two ends: W over 2 plus W, divided by 2.

117
00:13:25,544 --> 00:13:33,608
W over 2 plus W is 3 W over 2. Divided by 2, that is 3 quarters of W.

118
00:13:34,058 --> 00:13:39,434
So on average, 3 quarters of W bytes are in flight in each RTT.

119
00:13:39,884 --> 00:13:47,500
So the average TCP throughput is 3 quarters of W divided by RTT, in bytes per second.

120
00:13:47,950 --> 00:13:57,891
This is the answer to question 49 of the question bank. Question 49 has losses but no timeouts, so every loss gives this sawtooth.

121
00:13:58,341 --> 00:14:16,432
Is there a better way than AIMD to probe for bandwidth? TCP CUBIC uses this idea. W max is the sending rate at which the sender detected a congestion loss. The congestion state of the bottleneck link has probably not changed much since then.

122
00:14:16,882 --> 00:14:21,746
Classic TCP cuts the window in half, to W max over 2.

123
00:14:22,196 --> 00:14:28,831
Then classic TCP climbs back in a straight line, slowly, all the way to W max.

124
00:14:29,281 --> 00:14:36,363
CUBIC also cuts to W max over 2. But then it ramps up toward W max fast at first.

125
00:14:36,813 --> 00:14:41,891
Near W max, CUBIC slows down and approaches W max carefully.

126
00:14:42,341 --> 00:14:50,639
So CUBIC spends more time near W max. In this example, CUBIC has a higher throughput than classic TCP.

127
00:14:51,089 --> 00:15:00,647
Slide 88 gives the details. K is the point in time when the TCP window will reach W max. K itself is tunable.

128
00:15:01,097 --> 00:15:08,478
CUBIC increases W as a function of the cube of the distance between the current time, now, and K.

129
00:15:08,928 --> 00:15:13,024
Far from K, the cube is large, so the increase is large.

130
00:15:13,474 --> 00:15:20,663
Near K, the cube is small, so the increase is small and careful. The curve becomes flat at W max.

131
00:15:21,113 --> 00:15:29,263
After K, the distance grows again, so the window grows faster. CUBIC probes for more bandwidth above W max.

132
00:15:29,713 --> 00:15:41,041
Question 50 of the question bank asks for this cubic part. TCP CUBIC is the default in Linux, and the most popular TCP for popular Web servers.

133
00:15:41,491 --> 00:15:52,947
Now the exam question. This plot is from the fall 2016 final, on slide 89. Problem 4 of problem set 3 is the same question.

134
00:15:53,397 --> 00:15:59,242
The plot shows C wind, in segments, for each transmission round of TCP Reno.

135
00:15:59,692 --> 00:16:05,857
There are ten parts, a to j. Read each part, and use the plot. Pause the video and try it.

136
00:16:06,307 --> 00:16:12,558
Parts a and b. Use the shape of the curve. In slow start, C wind doubles each round.

137
00:16:13,008 --> 00:16:17,339
In congestion avoidance, C wind grows by 1 each round.

138
00:16:17,789 --> 00:16:25,362
From round 1 to round 6, C wind goes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32. It doubles each round.

139
00:16:25,812 --> 00:16:28,735
So slow start runs in rounds 1 to 6.

140
00:16:29,185 --> 00:16:36,011
From round 23 to round 26, C wind goes 1, 2, 4, 8. That is slow start again.

141
00:16:36,461 --> 00:16:45,144
From round 6 to round 16, C wind goes 32, 33, and up to 42. It grows by 1 each round.

142
00:16:45,594 --> 00:16:49,477
So congestion avoidance runs in rounds 6 to 16.

143
00:16:49,927 --> 00:17:03,068
From round 17 to round 22, C wind goes from 21 to 26, also by 1 each round. So congestion avoidance runs again in rounds 17 to 22.

144
00:17:03,518 --> 00:17:08,915
Part c. After round 16, C wind falls from 42 to 21.

145
00:17:09,365 --> 00:17:14,251
42 divided by 2 is 21. So C wind is cut in half.

146
00:17:14,701 --> 00:17:25,773
In Reno, only triple duplicate ACKs cut C wind in half. A timeout would set C wind to 1. So this loss was detected by triple duplicate ACKs.

147
00:17:26,223 --> 00:17:31,300
Part d. After round 22, C wind falls from 26 to 1.

148
00:17:31,750 --> 00:17:37,745
In Reno, a timeout sets C wind to 1. So this loss was detected by a timeout.

149
00:17:38,195 --> 00:17:49,843
The rule to remember. In Reno, triple duplicate ACKs set C wind to S S thresh, which is half of C wind. A timeout sets C wind to 1 MSS.

150
00:17:50,293 --> 00:17:54,794
Part e. Slow start stops when C wind reaches S S thresh.

151
00:17:55,244 --> 00:18:02,497
Here slow start stops at C wind 32, in round 6. So the initial S S thresh is 32.

152
00:18:02,947 --> 00:18:11,907
Part f. The loss after round 16 sets S S thresh to half of C wind just before the loss. C wind is 42 there.

153
00:18:12,357 --> 00:18:22,939
42 divided by 2 is 21. S S thresh stays 21 until the next loss. So in round 18, S S thresh is 21.

154
00:18:23,389 --> 00:18:30,706
Part g. The timeout after round 22 sets S S thresh to 26 over 2, which is 13.

155
00:18:31,156 --> 00:18:34,292
S S thresh stays 13 in round 24.

156
00:18:34,742 --> 00:18:46,369
Check the result. In rounds 23 to 26, C wind goes up to 8. 8 is less than 13. So the plot is still in slow start there, as part a says.

157
00:18:46,819 --> 00:18:58,424
Part h. In which round does the sender send segment 70? Each round sends C wind segments. Count them. Round 1 has C wind 1. It sends segment 1.

158
00:18:58,874 --> 00:19:02,842
Round 2 has C wind 2. It sends segments 2 and 3.

159
00:19:03,292 --> 00:19:07,580
Round 3 has C wind 4. It sends segments 4 to 7.

160
00:19:08,030 --> 00:19:12,361
Round 4 has C wind 8. It sends segments 8 to 15.

161
00:19:12,811 --> 00:19:18,080
Round 5 has C wind 16. It sends segments 16 to 31.

162
00:19:18,530 --> 00:19:23,927
Round 6 has C wind 32. It sends segments 32 to 63.

163
00:19:24,377 --> 00:19:32,527
So rounds 1 to 6 send 1 plus 2 plus 4 plus 8 plus 16 plus 32, which is 63 segments.

164
00:19:32,977 --> 00:19:42,939
Round 7 has C wind 33. It sends the next 33 segments: 64 to 63 plus 33, which is 96.

165
00:19:43,389 --> 00:19:49,213
Segment 70 is between 64 and 96. So the answer is round 7.

166
00:19:49,663 --> 00:20:03,167
The problem set solution writes 15 to 31 for round 5. It should be 16 to 31, because round 4 ends at segment 15. The answer, round 7, does not change.

167
00:20:03,617 --> 00:20:13,815
Part i. A loss after round 26 is detected by triple duplicate ACKs. Just before the loss, in round 26, C wind is 8.

168
00:20:14,265 --> 00:20:19,342
Reno sets S S thresh to C wind over 2. 8 over 2 is 4.

169
00:20:19,792 --> 00:20:28,837
Reno sets C wind to S S thresh. So C wind is 4, and S S thresh is 4. This matches the answer on the slide.

170
00:20:29,287 --> 00:20:37,629
Part j. Now use Tahoe, with triple duplicate ACKs at round 16. In round 16, C wind is 42.

171
00:20:38,079 --> 00:20:42,303
S S thresh becomes 42 over 2, which is 21.

172
00:20:42,753 --> 00:20:48,427
But Tahoe always sets C wind to 1 MSS. Then slow start runs again.

173
00:20:48,877 --> 00:20:56,835
C wind is 1 in round 17, 2 in round 18, and 4 in round 19. All are below 21.

174
00:20:57,285 --> 00:21:04,410
So at round 19, S S thresh is 21 and C wind is 4. This matches the slide answer.

175
00:21:04,860 --> 00:21:22,865
Problem 6 of problem set 3. There is no loss. TCP uses AIMD without slow start. C wind grows by 1 MSS each time a batch of ACKs arrives, that is, once per RTT. Pause the video and try it.

176
00:21:23,315 --> 00:21:27,219
Part a. C wind starts at 1 MSS, at time 0.

177
00:21:27,669 --> 00:21:36,992
It grows by 1 MSS each RTT: 2, 3, 4, 5. It reaches 6 MSS at time 5 RTT.

178
00:21:37,442 --> 00:21:48,493
From 1 to 6 is 6 minus 1, which is 5 steps of 1 MSS. Each step takes 1 RTT. So the answer is 5 RTT.

179
00:21:48,943 --> 00:21:57,241
Part b. In each RTT, the sender sends one window. In the first RTT, the window is 1 MSS.

180
00:21:57,691 --> 00:22:03,494
In the next RTTs, the sender sends 2, 3, 4 and 5 MSS.

181
00:22:03,944 --> 00:22:12,499
The total up to time 5 RTT is 1 plus 2 plus 3 plus 4 plus 5, which is 15 MSS.

182
00:22:12,949 --> 00:22:24,255
The average throughput is 15 MSS divided by 5 RTT. That is 3 MSS per RTT. This matches the problem set solution.

183
00:22:24,705 --> 00:22:31,831
The question bank also has descriptive questions, 43 to 46. Pause the video and try it.

184
00:22:32,281 --> 00:22:40,131
Question 43, the purpose: the sender must not send too fast for the network, that is, for the routers and the links.

185
00:22:40,581 --> 00:22:46,981
The mechanism is end-to-end. The sender keeps C wind and changes it when it detects a loss.

186
00:22:47,431 --> 00:23:00,551
Question 44 asks for the equation that joins congestion control and flow control. Last byte sent minus last byte acked is at most the minimum of C wind and R wind.

187
00:23:01,001 --> 00:23:13,140
Question 45 asks for the general idea of AIMD. Slow start begins at 1 MSS and doubles C wind every RTT, up to S S thresh.

188
00:23:13,590 --> 00:23:17,878
Then congestion avoidance adds 1 MSS per RTT.

189
00:23:18,328 --> 00:23:28,781
At a loss, S S thresh becomes C wind over 2. C wind becomes S S thresh or 1 MSS, as in the Tahoe and Reno table.

190
00:23:29,231 --> 00:23:37,146
Question 46 asks how the sender infers a loss. There are two ways: triple duplicate ACKs, and a timeout.

191
00:23:37,596 --> 00:23:42,844
The question bank has no published answers. These answers use the slides only.

192
00:23:43,294 --> 00:23:50,035
Question 48 asks how the value of S S thresh increases. Pause the video and try it.

193
00:23:50,485 --> 00:23:57,483
S S thresh does not grow round by round, as C wind does. It changes only at a loss event.

194
00:23:57,933 --> 00:24:01,111
In this example, S S thresh starts at 8.

195
00:24:01,561 --> 00:24:04,228
The first loss occurs at C wind 12.

196
00:24:04,678 --> 00:24:12,849
The new S S thresh is half of C wind just before the loss. 12 over 2 is 6. So S S thresh went down.

197
00:24:13,299 --> 00:24:17,715
Then C wind grows linearly for a long time, up to 22.

198
00:24:18,165 --> 00:24:24,842
The next loss sets S S thresh to 22 over 2, which is 11. So S S thresh went up.

199
00:24:25,292 --> 00:24:37,793
So S S thresh increases when the loss occurs at a C wind larger than 2 times the old S S thresh. Here 22 is larger than 2 times 6, which is 12.

200
00:24:38,243 --> 00:24:42,254
This example is ours. The rule is from slide 82.

201
00:24:42,704 --> 00:24:50,661
Summary. Congestion means long delays and packet loss in the routers. Congestion control is not flow control.

202
00:24:51,111 --> 00:25:02,461
TCP uses end-to-end control. The bytes in flight are at most the minimum of C wind and R wind. The rate is about C wind over RTT.

203
00:25:02,911 --> 00:25:09,908
Slow start begins at 1 MSS and doubles C wind every RTT, until S S thresh.

204
00:25:10,358 --> 00:25:14,305
Congestion avoidance adds 1 MSS per RTT.

205
00:25:14,755 --> 00:25:28,451
At a loss, S S thresh becomes C wind over 2. A timeout sets C wind to 1. Triple duplicate ACKs set the C wind of Reno to S S thresh, and the C wind of Tahoe to 1.

206
00:25:28,901 --> 00:25:40,143
The average Reno throughput is 3 quarters of W over RTT. CUBIC uses the cube of the distance to K. The next video starts the network layer.
